---
import ConverterPage from '../components/ConverterPage.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Which Hindi font converter do I actually need?',
    a: 'Work backwards from where the text is going. Going <strong>into</strong> a website, WhatsApp, email or a government portal, you need Unicode, so use <a href="/">Kruti Dev to Unicode</a>. Going <strong>into</strong> an old Word template or a form that must stay in Kruti Dev, you need the legacy keys, so use <a href="/unicode-to-kruti-dev">Unicode to Kruti Dev</a>.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Hindi the same as Unicode?',
    a: 'For this site, yes. Anything typed on a phone or on the Windows Hindi keypad is already Unicode Hindi. The two words describe the same text, which is why the boxes are labelled "Unicode / Hindi". Kruti Dev and DevLys are the exception: they are not Hindi at all, they are English keystrokes that a font draws as Hindi.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does my Kruti Dev text look like gibberish?',
    a: 'Because it is. A Kruti Dev document stores <code>ueksS</code>, not <span lang="hi">नमो</span>. The Hindi only appears when the Kruti Dev font is installed and applied. Open the same file on a machine without the font and you see the raw keystrokes. That is normal, and it is exactly what this converter translates.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between Mangal and Unicode?',
    a: 'None, at the level that matters. Mangal is a Unicode font: a Mangal document already contains real Devanagari characters. Converting "Kruti Dev to Mangal" and "Kruti Dev to Unicode" produces the same characters. The only difference is which font your document is set to display them in.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do Kruti Dev and DevLys use the same keyboard?',
    a: 'Almost. DevLys 010 and Kruti Dev 010 share nearly the whole layout, which is why DevLys is offered as a preview font on the Kruti Dev pages. "Almost" is not "exactly", though, so the DevLys converters stay marked coming soon until the differences have been checked against real DevLys documents.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is my text uploaded anywhere?',
    a: 'No. Every converter on this site runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you paste is sent to a server, logged or stored. You can disconnect from the internet after the page loads and it still works.',
  },
];
---

<ConverterPage
  path="/hindi-font-converter"
  lede="Convert Hindi between Kruti Dev, Mangal and Unicode, plus a plain guide to which one you need and why they are not the same thing."
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>Which converter do you need?</h2>
  <p>
    The converter above is the one most people arrive looking for: paste Kruti
    Dev, get Unicode Hindi you can use anywhere. If that is not your direction,
    every conversion has its own page, so you pick the conversion by picking the
    page rather than wrestling with a dropdown.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/">Kruti Dev to Unicode</a>: an old Word file into Hindi that
      works on the web, in WhatsApp and on government portals.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/unicode-to-kruti-dev">Unicode to Kruti Dev</a>: Hindi you typed
      on a keypad back into Kruti Dev keys, for a form or template that still
      demands the legacy font.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/kruti-dev-to-mangal">Kruti Dev to Mangal</a>: the same Unicode
      output, labelled for people whose documents are set in Mangal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/mangal-to-kruti-dev">Mangal to Kruti Dev</a>: the reverse.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/all-converters">All converters</a>: the full list, including
      the ones still being verified.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Hindi, Unicode and Mangal are the same thing</h2>
  <p>
    Text typed on a phone or on the Windows Hindi keypad <strong
      >is already Unicode</strong
    >. The characters are real Devanagari: <span lang="hi">न</span> is stored as
    the letter <span lang="hi">न</span>, and any font can draw it. Mangal is
    simply one such font, shipped with Windows. So "Hindi", "Unicode" and
    "Mangal" describe one kind of text with three names, and this site labels
    the boxes "Unicode / Hindi" so both audiences recognise their own word.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is also why there is no separate "Hindi to Mangal" page. There would be
    nothing for it to do.
  </p>

  <h2>Kruti Dev and DevLys are not Hindi</h2>
  <p>
    A legacy font works the other way round. Kruti Dev does not store Devanagari
    at all. It stores ordinary English letters and punctuation, and the font
    file draws a Hindi glyph in place of each one. The word <span lang="hi"
      >नमो</span
    > is stored as the four characters <code>ueksS</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two consequences follow, and between them they explain almost every problem
    people bring to a converter:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Without the font, the text is unreadable.</strong> Send a Kruti Dev
      document to someone who does not have Kruti Dev installed and they see the
      raw keystrokes. Paste it into a website and it stays gibberish forever,
      because a web page cannot borrow a font from your hard disk.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing can search or spell-check it.</strong> To a computer it is
      English nonsense. Search, sorting, screen readers and translation all fail.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Converting to Unicode fixes both permanently. That is the direction worth
    taking if you have a choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Working out which font an old document used</h2>
  <p>
    Open the file and look at the font name in the toolbar. That is usually the
    fastest answer. If the file has lost its formatting, the keystrokes
    themselves are a clue: Kruti Dev and DevLys 010 share almost the same
    layout, so <code>fgUnh</code> reading as <span lang="hi">हिन्दी</span> points
    at one of those two. Chanakya and Shusha use genuinely different layouts and
    will not decode correctly with a Kruti Dev map, which is why their pages stay
    marked coming soon rather than returning plausible-looking rubbish.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the text already looks like Hindi on a machine with no special fonts
    installed, it is Unicode and needs no conversion at all.
  </p>
</ConverterPage>
